What: on macOS, libnio advertises openat support (and so Files.newDirectoryStream returns a
  SecureDirectoryStream) only when libSystem itself exports renameat, checked at run time with
  dlopen("/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib", RTLD_NOLOAD) and dlsym. On 10.9 SecureDirectoryStream is
  off; the same binary keeps it on 10.10 and later, where libSystem has renameat.
Why on 10.9: 10.9 has no *at calls; the JDK gets them from Recaulk (macports-legacy-support
  1.5.2). Its renameat, when the two directory descriptors differ and both names are relative,
  turns each descriptor into a path with fcntl(F_GETPATH) and calls rename(path, path). That is
  the call UnixSecureDirectoryStream.move makes (renameat(this.dfd, from, that.dfd, to)), so a
  parent of either side swapped for a symlink between F_GETPATH and rename redirects the move
  outside the directories the caller opened: the race SecureDirectoryStream exists to rule out.
Provenance: ours. It replaces Hiker159/jdk-macos-legacy@70d25ea1d6f7292c8e926e9efe112f949800ae10,
  patches/25/mavericks.patch, src/java.base/unix/native/libnio/fs/UnixNativeDispatcher.c hunk
  3/6, which turned SecureDirectoryStream off by resolving openat/fstatat/fdopendir with dlsym;
  with Recaulk linked those resolve, so that would not keep it off, and it was off on 10.10+ too.
Exit: Recaulk gains an fd-relative renameat, then delete this patch.

--- a/src/java.base/unix/native/libnio/fs/UnixNativeDispatcher.c
+++ b/src/java.base/unix/native/libnio/fs/UnixNativeDispatcher.c
@@ -367,6 +367,18 @@
         my_fstatat_func = (fstatat_func*)&fstatat_wrapper;
 #endif
 
+#if defined(__APPLE__)
+    {
+        void* libsystem = dlopen("/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib", RTLD_LAZY | RTLD_NOLOAD);
+        if (libsystem == NULL || dlsym(libsystem, "renameat") == NULL) {
+            my_renameat_func = NULL;
+        }
+        if (libsystem != NULL) {
+            dlclose(libsystem);
+        }
+    }
+#endif
+
     /* supports openat, etc. */
 
     if (my_openat_func != NULL &&  my_fstatat_func != NULL &&
